Let’s turn the toothbrush into a living, responsive interface. Meet the 'Chemo-Optical Pulse.' This innovation uses a biodegradable polymer brush head infused with a specific engineered enzyme that reacts to the presence of organic debris. When food particles hit the bristles, the enzyme catalyzes a rapid, localized release of a fluorescent dye. Simultaneously, the bristle tips contain micro-photocatalytic cells that activate only when exposed to the specific light spectrum emitted by the dye. This creates a self-amplifying feedback loop: the more debris, the brighter the signal, the stronger the photocatalytic breakdown. The mechanism involves breaking down the molecular bonds of the biofilm through targeted photon absorption, effectively dissolving the problem at the source
